## Solution

We evaluate $\int_C \frac{1}{z^{2017}-1}\,dz$ where $C: |z|=2$ (counterclockwise), using the residue-at-infinity formula
$$\int_C f(z)\,dz = 2\pi i\,\operatorname{Res}\!\left(\frac{1}{z^{2}}f\!\left(\frac{1}{z}\right);\,0\right).$$

**Step 1. Compute the transformed integrand.**

With $f(z)=\dfrac{1}{z^{2017}-1}$,
$$\frac{1}{z^{2}}f\!\left(\frac{1}{z}\right)=\frac{1}{z^{2}}\cdot\frac{1}{z^{-2017}-1}=\frac{1}{z^{2}}\cdot\frac{z^{2017}}{1-z^{2017}}=\frac{z^{2015}}{1-z^{2017}}.$$

So we need $\operatorname{Res}\!\left(\dfrac{z^{2015}}{1-z^{2017}};\,0\right)$.

**Step 2. Laurent expansion at the origin.**

The function $\dfrac{1}{1-z^{2017}}$ is analytic at $0$ with Taylor series
$$\frac{1}{1-z^{2017}}=1+z^{2017}+z^{4034}+z^{6051}+\cdots=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}z^{2017k}.$$

Multiplying by $z^{2015}$:
$$\frac{z^{2015}}{1-z^{2017}}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}z^{2015+2017k}=z^{2015}+z^{4032}+z^{6049}+\cdots.$$

**Step 3. Extract the residue.**

The residue at $0$ is the coefficient of $z^{-1}$. We need
$$2015+2017k=-1 \iff 2017k=-2016 \iff k=-\frac{2016}{2017}.$$

Since $2016/2017$ is not an integer, **no term in the series equals $z^{-1}$**. Hence
$$\operatorname{Res}\!\left(\frac{z^{2015}}{1-z^{2017}};\,0\right)=0.$$

**Step 4. Conclude.**

$$\int_C \frac{1}{z^{2017}-1}\,dz = 2\pi i\cdot 0 = 0.$$

**Verification (independent check).** The poles of $f$ are the $2017$th roots of unity $\omega_0,\dots,\omega_{2016}$, all lying on $|z|=1$, hence all inside $|z|=2$. At each simple pole $\omega_j$,
$$\operatorname{Res}(f,\omega_j)=\frac{1}{2017\,\omega_j^{2016}}=\frac{\omega_j}{2017},$$
using $\omega_j^{2017}=1\Rightarrow\omega_j^{-2016}=\omega_j$. Therefore
$$\sum_{j=0}^{2016}\operatorname{Res}(f,\omega_j)=\frac{1}{2017}\sum_{j=0}^{2016}\omega_j=\frac{1}{2017}\cdot 0=0,$$
since the sum of all $2017$th roots of unity is $0$. Thus $\int_C f\,dz=2\pi i\cdot 0=0$, confirming the result.

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\int_C \frac{1}{z^{2017}-1}\,dz=0}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
